      {/* THE CAPTION IS A PARAGRAPH, NOT A <caption>, AND THAT IS THE BUG FIX.
          It was a real <caption> inside the table. Below 700px the table becomes a stack of
          cards, which is done by setting display:block on the table and its rows and cells. A
          <caption> keeps display:table-caption, so once its parent stopped being a table the
          browser wrapped it in an anonymous table box sized to its own min-content, and the
          caption rendered one word per line down the page. Resetting the caption's display
          would have fixed the symptom; taking it out of the table means table layout can never
          reach it again, at any width. */}
      <p class="bt-cap">Rate parity by jurisdiction, with the ruling or statute behind each row. Checked {bookingTacticsChecked}. {parityLinked} of the {parityRows.length} rows link the primary document, and the {parityRows.length - parityLinked} that do not are named as unlinked rather than left to look sourced. {flaggedCount} rows are a single country, and the {linkedCount} of those with a guide on this site link to it.</p>

      <div class="bt-tablewrap">
        <table class="bt-table">
          {/* Explicit column widths, because without a colgroup the browser sized all four from
              their content and the ruling column, the one a reader is actually here for, was
              crushed into a strip while three short columns took the room.

              RETUNED FROM 18/14/12/56. At 18% the jurisdiction column was wider than any
              country name needs, so every row opened with an empty band on the left. It is now
              14%, and the 4 points go to the ruling. Note that the th's old min-width:130px was
              doing the same job as the colgroup and fighting it: 130px IS 18% of this table, so
              the column could not narrow until that came off too. It has.

              table-layout stays auto, so a long jurisdiction name can still push its own column
              rather than overflowing it. */}
          <colgroup>
            <col style="width:19%" />
            <col style="width:17%" />
            <col style="width:14%" />
            <col style="width:50%" />
          </colgroup>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th scope="col">Jurisdiction</th>
              <th scope="col">Status</th>
              <th scope="col">Since</th>
              <th scope="col">What changed, and the source</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {parityRows.map((r) => (
              <tr id={parityRowId(r)} class:list={[{ 'is-us': r.status === 'binds' }]}>
                {/* A flag appears wherever the row is one country we can identify. A LINK
                    appears only where there is a guide behind it, so Belgium shows its flag
                    with a plain name. A multi-country row gets neither: "EU and EEA, all 27
                    member states" cannot wear one flag without asserting something false. */}
                <th scope="row" data-l="Jurisdiction">
                  {r.guideUrl ? (
                    <a class="bt-j" href={r.guideUrl}>
                      <span class="bt-jflag"><img src={r.flag} alt="" width="22" height="16" loading="lazy" /></span>
                      <span class="bt-jname">{r.jurisdiction}</span>
                    </a>
                  ) : (
                    <span class="bt-j is-plain">
                      {r.flag && <span class="bt-jflag"><img src={r.flag} alt="" width="22" height="16" loading="lazy" /></span>}
                      <span class="bt-jname">{r.jurisdiction}</span>
                    </span>
                  )}
                </th>
                <td data-l="Status">
                  <span class={`bt-chip is-${PARITY_STATUS[r.status].tone}`}>{PARITY_STATUS[r.status].label}</span>
                </td>
                <td data-l="Since" class="bt-since">{r.sinceDate}</td>
                <td data-l="What changed">
                  <span class="bt-ruling">{r.ruling}</span>
                  {/* THE NEGATIVE CLAIM SHOWS ITS METHOD. Every other row points at a document
                      that exists; this one asserts that a document does not, and there is no
                      URL for that. Left as a bare label it read as the one row nobody had to
                      check, which is the opposite of what it is: it is the row a US reader
                      most needs and the one most likely to go out of date, because it would
                      take a single state legislature. So it renders a dated method, what
                      would overturn it, and the hole in it, rather than a citation. */}
                  {r.methodology && (
                    <div class="bt-meth">
                      <span class="bt-meth-k">How a negative was checked</span>
                      <p>{r.methodology.how}</p>
                      <p><b>What would change this:</b> {r.methodology.wouldChangeThis}</p>
                      <p class="bt-meth-gap">{r.methodology.inventoryPending}</p>
                      <p class="bt-meth-d">Stated as of {r.methodology.cutoff}.</p>
                    </div>
                  )}
                  {r.source.url
                    ? <a class="bt-tsrc" href={r.source.url} target="_blank" rel="noopener">{r.source.label}</a>
                    : <span class="bt-tsrc bt-nosrc">{r.source.label}</span>}
                </td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="bt-do">Two things this table does not say. It does not say a direct rate will be cheaper, only that a hotel is free to make it so. And it says nothing about whether the price you were shown had to include the mandatory fees, which is a separate question answered by <a href="#drip">the disclosure rules above</a>. A jurisdiction can be strict on one and silent on the other, and the US is exactly that: no parity ban, and one of the more specific fee-disclosure rules anywhere.</p>

